<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Han Shantong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1351">1351</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Han Shantong was a Chinese leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus">White Lotus</a> sect from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> who preached the imminent coming of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Buddha Maitreya</a> and claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> emperors. In 1351 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-futong">Liu Futong</a> planned an uprising against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, but the plot was discovered and he was captured and executed. His followers launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a> was proclaimed emperor of the Song in 1355.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Han Shantong was born in Luancheng, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, and claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Emperor Huizong</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> (1082–1135), the penultimate emperor of the Northern Song dynasty. His following grew out of the hardship after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> flood of 1344, which caused heavy casualties, displacement, hunger and epidemics and brought crisis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, the Yuan capital. Popular discontent with the Mongol-led Yuan dynasty came to a head with the great construction project of rerouting the Yellow River in 1351.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the works Han claimed to have found a stone image with only one eye and a prophecy of revolt inscribed on its back, an account recorded in the Caomuzi that probably reflects beliefs already widespread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>. He proclaimed the rebirth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya Buddha</a> and the near coming of the King of Light, and apparently took the title himself: no record says so, but after his death his son was known as the King of Light the Young. Liu Futong became his staunch supporter. When their plot was discovered, Han was executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a> became the symbol of the rebellion that followed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-futong">Liu Futong</a> as its leading general, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, the future founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, was still Han Lin'er's subordinate as late as 1363. Although the military power of Han and Liu declined after 1359, Lin'er was probably seen as a legitimate future emperor until he drowned in a pond in 1366 or 1367. It has been suspected that the drowning was staged by Zhu Yuanzhang's agents, who was by then close to victory.</p><p>Historical sources on the years before the founding of the Ming were destroyed or heavily censored to hide the founding emperor's humble origins and his ties to the rebels, so information on Han Shantong and other rebel leaders is very scarce.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Han Shantong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Han Shantong</b>, a White Lotus leader who preached the coming of the Buddha Maitreya and claimed descent from the Song emperors, launched the Red Turban revolt against the Yuan Dynasty in 1351 but was killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/han-song">Han Song</a></li></ul></section>
